During a code review for a regional bank's incident automation, you find this server-side snippet in a Script Include. The author says it "works in dev". Walk through what each marked line actually returns or does in production, and which lines are bugs waiting to happen.<br><pre>var inc = new GlideRecord('incident');
var found = inc.get('INC0010023');            // (a)
var ref = inc.getValue('u_vendor_ticket');     // (b) field is empty on this record
if (ref.length &gt; 0) { /* ... */ }
if (inc.getValue('active') === true) { /* ... */ }  // (c)
var callers = [];
// later, inside a while(gr.next()) loop over 50 incidents:
callers.push(gr.caller_id);                    // (d)
var upd = new GlideRecord('incident');
upd.get(staleSysId);                           // (e) record was deleted last week
upd.setValue('short_description', 'Re-routed');
upd.update();</pre>

During a code review at a telecom provider you find this onChange client script on the Incident <code>caller_id</code> field. Agents complain that the form freezes for a second or two every time they pick a caller, and that opening an existing incident is slow.<pre>function onChange(control, oldValue, newValue, isLoading) {
  var caller = g_form.getReference('caller_id');
  if (caller.vip == 'true') {
    g_form.addDecoration('caller_id', 'icon-star', 'VIP');
  }
  g_form.setValue('location', caller.location);
}</pre>Explain everything wrong with it, what <code>getReference()</code> actually does with and without a callback, and rewrite it.

A university's IT team built a Service Portal widget called "My open requests" for 38,000 students. In code review you find this server script:<pre>(function() {
  data.items = [];
  var gr = new GlideRecord('sc_req_item');
  gr.addQuery('request.requested_for', $sp.getParameter('user'));
  gr.addQuery('active', true);
  gr.query();
  while (gr.next()) {
    var item = {};
    $sp.getRecordValues(item, gr, 'number,cat_item,state,sys_updated_on');
    data.items.push(item);
  }
})();</pre>Students say the list shows 32-character codes instead of item names and a number instead of the state, and a student has already shown that changing <code>?user=</code> in the URL lists a classmate's requests. <strong>Question:</strong> Explain everything wrong with this script, how <code>data</code>, <code>input</code> and <code>options</code> flow between client and server, and rewrite it.

A logistics company's scoped app keeps a vendor message log with about 9 million rows. A developer wrote this scheduled job to delete processed messages older than 90 days, tested it on a dev instance with 300 rows, and promoted it. The first production run deleted 4.1 million rows, including unprocessed messages still awaiting retry. Find every problem in the script, explain why the platform behaved this way, and rewrite it safely.<br><pre>var log = new GlideRecord('x_acme_vlog_message');
log.addEncodedQuery('u_statsu=processed^sys_created_on&lt;javascript:gs.daysAgoStart(90)');
log.setLimit(5000);   // "safety net"
log.query();
log.deleteMultiple();</pre>

An airline's ops team asks why their "active critical VPN incidents" report script never returns a single P1 incident, even though the list view shows several. The developer meant: active AND (priority 1 OR priority 2) AND short description contains VPN AND state is not 6 or 7. Tell me what this script actually filters on, list each bug, and give both a GlideRecord and a GlideQuery version that produce the intended logic.<br><pre>var gr = new GlideRecord('incident');
gr.addQuery('active', true).addOrCondition('priority', 1);
gr.addQuery('priority', 2);
gr.addQuery('short_description', 'contains', 'VPN');
var st = gr.addQuery('state', '!=', 6);
st.addCondition('state', 'NOT IN', ['7']);
gr.query();</pre>

A managed service provider runs routing rules for 30 customers. Each rule is a record in <code>x_msp_route_rule</code> with an encoded condition (built by service owners in a condition builder) and a target group. An after-update business rule on incident evaluates the rules. Since go-live, customers report missed routes for lower-case company names, routes firing for incidents with an empty due date, and the "assigned" event firing twice. Review this business rule and explain each symptom.<br><pre>(function executeRule(current, previous) {
  if (current.assigned_to.changes()) {
    gs.eventQueue('incident.assigned', current, current.assigned_to, previous.assigned_to);
  }
  var rule = new GlideRecord('x_msp_route_rule');
  rule.addActiveQuery();
  rule.query();
  while (rule.next()) {
    if (GlideFilter.checkRecord(current, rule.getValue('u_condition'))) {
      current.assignment_group = rule.getValue('u_group');
      current.update();
      break;
    }
  }
})(current, previous);</pre>

A software reseller tracks licence seats in a scoped table, <code>x_acme_seat_pool</code>, with an integer field <code>u_seats_used</code>. A Scripted REST endpoint is called by a provisioning tool that runs 20 parallel workers. After a busy Monday, finance finds the pool shows 812 seats used while there are 861 active assignment records. Here is the increment code. Explain what is wrong, fix it, and explain how you would still enforce the pool's seat cap.<br><pre>var pool = new GlideRecord('x_acme_seat_pool');
if (pool.get(poolId)) {
  var used = parseInt(pool.getValue('u_seats_used'), 10);
  pool.setValue('u_seats_used', used + 1);
  pool.update();
}</pre>

A university's HR team built a Service Portal widget for staff to search their own HR cases. Security review flags the server script below: a staff member changed the filter in the browser and saw case titles belonging to other people, and also inferred salary bands by filtering on a restricted field and watching which results came back. Explain exactly what each API does and does not enforce, and rewrite the server side so it respects ACLs, including query ACLs.<br><pre>var gr = new GlideRecord('x_univ_hr_case');
gr.addEncodedQuery(input.filter);   // filter comes from the browser
gr.setLimit(50);
gr.query();
while (gr.next()) {
  data.rows.push({ number: gr.getValue('number'),
                   title: gr.getValue('short_description'),
                   band: gr.getValue('u_salary_band') });
}</pre>

A contractor wrote a two-way sync between ServiceNow change requests and a partner's release calendar. In production, change start dates drift by the integration user's UTC offset each time a record is round-tripped, some changes lost their assignment group, a test run once updated every change on the instance, and the partner keeps receiving 404s for changes that exist. Review this Node.js and tell me every defect, why it happens according to the Table API reference, and the corrected version.<pre>const base = INSTANCE + '/api/now/table/change_request';
const q = 'statte=scheduled^ORDERBYnumber';
let off = 0, page;
do {
  page = (await get(`${base}?sysparm_query=${q}&amp;sysparm_display_value=true&amp;sysparm_limit=500&amp;sysparm_offset=${off}`)).result;
  for (const chg of page) {
    const cal = partner.lookup(chg.number);
    if (!cal) continue;
    chg.start_date = cal.start ?? chg.start_date;
    await put(`${base}/${chg.sys_id}`, chg);
  }
  off += 500;
} while (page.length === 500);</pre>
